<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Lament · 1916 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Isa Lei</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Fijian farewell song from the 1910s, sung when friends part. Its tune may have come from a Tongan love song.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>disputed: Ratu Tevita Uluilakeba by one account; Fijian words to a Tongan love song by another</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>disputed: Ratu Tevita Uluilakeba by one account; a Tongan melody of 1915 by another</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Fijians; Tongans</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Fijian, Tongan</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Fiji (Lakeba or Suva by one account; from Tonga by another)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1915–1918 (accounts differ)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional Fijian farewell song, of 1915–1918 by the accounts of its origin)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-lei">Isa Lei</a>&quot; is a traditional Fijian farewell song, and its origin is disputed. By one account, Ratu Tevita Uluilakeba, the Turaga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> na Tu'i Nayau, composed it in 1916 for Adi Litia Tavanavanua (1900–1983) when she visited Tubou, on Lakeba. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> Museum holds Uluilakeba's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, but by the museum's description he composed the song in 1918, while he was training as a civil servant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suva">Suva</a>. Uluilakeba was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratu-sir-kamisese-mara">Ratu Sir Kamisese Mara</a>, the founding father of modern Fiji.</p><p>By another account, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-lei">Isa Lei</a>&quot; is the Fijian version of a Tongan love song written in 1915 to court the then Princess Salote, later Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salote-tupou-iii">Sālote Tupou III</a>. A visiting Fijian sergeant heard it, and the Fijians turned it into a farewell song while keeping the Tongan melody.</p><p>In 1932 Lieutenant A. W. Caten, a bandmaster of the Fijian Defence Forces, made a foxtrot arrangement of the song. He is often credited on modern recordings of it, among them versions by The Seekers and by Ry Cooder and V. M. Bhatt on their album A Meeting by the River. The Fijian singer Sakiusa Bulicokocoko performed it regularly.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A farewell song of parting and of the wish to be remembered, drawn by one account from a love song. Its mood is tender and wistful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Fijian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Fijian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Isa! Isa! vulagi lasa dina</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Isa! Isa! visitor who truly gladdened [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Nomu lako au rarawa kina</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your going, I grieve over it.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">A'cava beka ko a mai cakava?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What was it, perhaps, that you came to do [?]?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Na nomu lako, au na sega ni lasa.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>At your going I shall not be happy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Isa Lei na noqu rarawa,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Isa Lei, my sorrow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Ni ko sa na vodo ena mataka</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>when you will go aboard in the morning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Bau nanuma tiko ga,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>just keep remembering,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Mai Suva, nanuma tiko ga.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>keep remembering Suva.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Vanua rogo na nomuni Vanua</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A famous land is your land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Kena ca ni levu tu na ua</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its one fault is that the waves are big;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Lomaqu voli me'u bau butuka</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my heart longs [?] that I might tread on it,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Tovolea ke balavu na bula.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I will try, if life is long.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Vanua rogo na nomu yanuyanu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A famous land is your island,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Kena kau wale na Salusalu,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its plants are nothing but garlands [?]:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Mocelolo, Bua, na Kukuwalu,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>mocelolo, bua, kukuwalu,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fj">Lagakali, baba na rosi damu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>lagakali, and beside them the red rose.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional; the Fijian text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isa_Lei" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1 and 2.1: 'Isa' (fuller 'Isa lei') is a cry of longing and sorrow, kept as it stands; 'vulagi' is a visitor or stranger, 'lasa' joy or delight, 'dina' true: the phrase is read here as 'a visitor who truly brought joy', but the grammar is uncertain.</li><li>line 1.3: 'A cava beka', 'what, perhaps'; the line reads as a reproach, 'why ever did you come?'.</li><li>line 2.2: 'vodo', to board a ship or vehicle.</li><li>line 2.4: 'Mai Suva' could also be 'here at Suva', the singer's place.</li><li>line 3.3: 'Lomaqu voli', 'my wish, my longing'; 'voli' is uncertain here.</li><li>line 4.2: 'kau' is tree or plant; 'wale', merely; 'salusalu', garland: the island's plants are all fit for garlands.</li><li>lines 4.3-4.4: mocelolo, bua (frangipani), kukuwalu and lagakali are fragrant Fijian flowering plants used in garlands; they are kept as names. 'baba' is the side or edge.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
